Ans. Option 1: Relevance Groups with Match Rules (Recommended)
This is the cleanest approach because it keeps your physical territory structure intact while using metadata to steer the FSL scheduling engine.

Tag the Service Resources:

Add a custom picklist or text field on ServiceResource called Partner_Affiliation__c (Values: Partner A, Partner B).

Tag all technicians belonging to Contractor A as Partner A, and Contractor B as Partner B.

Mirror the Queue on the Service Appointment:

Create a field on ServiceAppointment called Assigned_Partner__c.

When a Work Order or Service Appointment is assigned to Queue A, a simple Record-Triggered Flow populates Assigned_Partner__c = 'Partner A'.

When assigned to Queue B, it populates Assigned_Partner__c = 'Partner B'.

Configure a Match Fields Work Rule:

Create an FSL Match Fields Work Rule:

Service Appointment Field: Assigned_Partner__c

Service Resource Field: Partner_Affiliation__c

Add this rule to your active Scheduling Policy.

Result:

When the dispatcher or auto-scheduling engine runs for a Service Appointment from Queue A, the engine filters out all of Partner B's resources and only considers Partner A's technicians, even though they share the exact same geographic boundary.


